course: dictionary
lesson: 4496
title: Sitting on your haunches
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A haunch is the hip, bottom and top of the leg of a person or animal. When a creature sits back on its haunches, its weight rests on its back legs and hips. Read the clues around the word. «The Elephant's Child sat back on his little haunches, and pulled, and pulled, and pulled» shows that he is sitting low and leaning back, not standing up.
explain: Look at what the animal is doing. Pulling hard, watching or looking around are all clues that the body is low and braced. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «sat back on his hips» still makes sense, your meaning fits.
explain: The word also means meat. «A haunch of venison» is the back leg and hip of a deer, cooked as food. Venison means deer meat, so the clue is in the second word. The two meanings are linked, because both are about the back part of an animal's leg.
fact: A haunch is the hip, bottom and top of the leg.
fact: «Sat back on its haunches» means crouched low with the weight on the back legs.
fact: In stories, a dog, rabbit or hare often sits on its haunches to look around.
fact: Rudyard Kipling's animals often sit on their haunches, as in «The Elephant's Child».
fact: In old feasts, «a haunch of venison» meant the back leg and hip of a deer, roasted as meat.
exercise: «He sat back on his haunches to watch the rabbit.» What does sat back on his haunches mean? | Jumped high into the air | *Crouched low, with his weight on his back legs | Ran away as fast as he could | Lay flat on his stomach
exercise: Dad carved «a haunch of venison» for the feast. What was carved? | *The back leg and hip of a deer, cooked as meat | A type of bread | A loud shout | A small wooden chair
exercise: «The Elephant's Child sat back on his little haunches, and pulled, and pulled.» What was his body doing? | *Sitting low and leaning back on his back legs | Flying over the trees | Swimming in the river | Standing tall with his trunk in the air
test: «The squirrel squatted on its haunches and nibbled a nut.» What does squatted on its haunches show? | Standing tall on its front paws | Hopping across the lawn | *Crouching low on its back legs | Asleep in a tree
test: Old feasts often had «a haunch of venison» roasted. What is a haunch here? | A whole tall tree | A stone wall | A bag of flour | *A back leg and hip of a deer, as meat
exam: Ella says a haunch is an animal's ear. What is the correct meaning? | An animal's ear | A kind of hat | A small round stone | *The hip, bottom and top of the leg, where an animal sits back
